[Start "quiet-setups 2.Nf3 Nf6"]

3. e3 e6 { Opens the bishop on f8 and holds d5. The bishop on c8 is shut in for now; it comes out after a pawn trade or ...b6. } 4. Bd3 c5 { Hits d4 before White has played c3 or e4. Trading White's d-pawn leaves White with little space. } 5. c3 ( 5. b3 Nc6 6. O-O Bd6 7. Bb2 O-O 8. Nbd2 cxd4 { Takes White's d-pawn off the board. After exd4 the d4 pawn is a target for ...Nb4 and ...b6 with ...Bb7. } 9. exd4 b6 ) ( 5. dxc5 Bxc5 { Takes the pawn straight back; the bishop now eyes f2. } 6. O-O O-O 7. c3 Nc6 ) ( 5. Nbd2 Nc6 6. c3 Bd6 ) 5... Nc6 { A second attacker on d4, and another piece developed. } 6. Nbd2 ( 6. O-O Bd6 7. Nbd2 O-O ) 6... Bd6 { Develops towards h2 and gets ready to castle. The knight on f6 covers h7, so Bxh7+ does not work. } 7. O-O O-O { Castles before the centre opens. A king left in the middle is the usual way to lose quickly from these quiet positions. } 8. dxc5 ( 8. e4 cxd4 { Counter-attack in the centre: if White recaptures, the e4 pawn loses its support from d3-c3. } 9. cxd4 dxe4 10. Nxe4 Nxe4 11. Bxe4 h6 ) 8... Bxc5 { Wins the pawn back, and the bishop now looks at f2 and the whole a7-g1 diagonal. } 9. e4 Qc7 { Waits instead of trading on e4, where the exchanges favour White. The queen supports ...e5 and watches h2. } 10. Qe2 h6 { Gives the king a flight square and stops Ng5 or Bg5 jumping at f6 and h7. } 11. b4 Bd6 { The bishop steps out of the pawn's attack and still points at h2. } 12. Bb2 Rd8 { Plan: rooks to d8 and e8, then ...e5 or ...dxe4 when White's pieces are loose. If White plays e5, hit it with ...Nd7 and ...f6 rather than moving the knight to the edge. } *
